title: The non-finite guard is too wide for a float carrying a %GO_CONSTANT token
updated: 2026-08-21T05:47:37Z
---

Found while implementing karr #105 / ADR 0026 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops).

karr #59's guard in Encrypted::assert_representable refuses every non-finite
float on the encrypt path. Its stated premise, from the ADR 0013 measurement:
YAML::XS writes such a value as a bare `Inf` / `-Inf` / `NaN`, which go-yaml
resolves as a STRING, so the document fails its own MAC (sops -d exit 51).

That premise holds for a bare NV. It does NOT hold for a float whose string
half is one of the twelve %GO_CONSTANT tokens -- the shape ADR 0026 now
produces at parse time for a document sops wrote:

  YAML::XS Dump of dualvar(+Inf,  ".inf")  -> `.inf`
  YAML::XS Dump of dualvar(-Inf, "-.inf")  -> `-.inf`
  YAML::XS Dump of dualvar(NaN,   ".nan")  -> `.nan`

Go resolves each of those back to the same double and digests +Inf / -Inf /
NaN, which is exactly what value_to_bytes derives from the numeric half. The
document agrees with its own MAC.

MEASURED, with the guard bypassed IN A SCRATCH PROCESS ONLY, no code changed:
File::SOPS->encrypt of { keep => "x", v_unencrypted => dualvar($inf, $token) }
writes the token bare and `sops -d` reads it back at exit 0 -- five tokens
tried (.inf -.inf .nan .Inf +.INF), five exit 0, the value correct in each.
ADR 0013's own emit-side guard never even sees such a leaf: canonical_float_tree
returns a non-finite float unchecked, so nothing else is in the way.

CONSEQUENCE TODAY: ADR 0026 made those documents READABLE, and they still
cannot be written back. rotate and edit croak from this guard -- naming the key
path now, which is better than the MAC error they used to give, but with advice
("store the value as a string") that leads straight back to ADR 0013's
refusal, because the string .inf is written bare in YAML.

WHAT THIS TICKET IS NOT: a request to drop the guard. It is right about every
value it was written for, and a caller's own 9**9**9 must keep croaking. The
question is whether it should ask for a string half that a foreign reader
resolves back to the same double before refusing -- and that is format-specific
(the JSON emitter writes null for all three), so the answer may be that the
check belongs beside ADR 0013's in Format::YAML rather than in the format-blind
assert_representable.

Needs its own corpus and its own ADR. karr #105 said in as many words not to
take it there. Wire lane. Needs the sops binary to prove.
