THE PREDICATE: a non-finite float is refused unless _has_public_pv says it
carries its own string half AND _go_non_finite_token_bytes maps that half to
the same bytes value_to_bytes derives from its number. dualvar(+Inf,'-.inf') is
a contradiction and stays refused, as ADR 0012 does for an integer. It is a
GATE: the verdict stays with ADR 0013's guard in Format::YAML, which compares
the token the emitter really writes against _go_scalar_bytes -- measured
directly, that guard accepts .inf/+Inf, refuses all three contradictions and
.INf, and ACCEPTS dualvar(+Inf,'banana'), which is why the gate has to name the
twelve tokens rather than just ask for a PV.

JSON: measured, it fails in BOTH slots -- unencrypted sops -d exit 51 (Cpanel
writes a quoted ".inf" while the digest covers +Inf), encrypted exit 4 (Go
cannot marshal a non-finite float64). assert_representable cannot see the
format, so the exemption is closed downstream: canonical_float_tree refuses a
deferred leaf in a document that carries a MAC when the handler installed no
reject_scalar, which is JSON and only JSON (the plaintext emitters install none
and need none, so decrypt_file still writes .inf). MAC coverage is read off the
tree's top-level sops key, the same fact ADR 0026's walk keys on. The encrypted
slot keeps its refusal in encrypt_value, in both formats -- karr #122 for the
YAML half it costs.

